<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Lakhmid Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">300 CE – 602 CE (approx.) · capital al-Hira</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Arab client kingdom of the Sasanians at al-Hira, a centre of early Arabic poetry and Christianity on the Euphrates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lakhmids, or Nasrids, established themselves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hira">al-Hira</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in the late 3rd century as allies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namara-inscription">Namara inscription</a> of 328 commemorates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imru-al-qays-ibn-amr">Imru al-Qays</a>, son of Amr, as king of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>; he is usually counted as an early Lakhmid ruler.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hira">al-Hira</a> the kings controlled the Arab tribes of the desert fringe for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ghassanid-kingdom">Ghassanid Kingdom</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium's</a> behalf. al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mundhir-iii">Mundhir III</a> raided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> repeatedly in the 6th century. al-Hira was a cosmopolitan town with a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> community, monasteries and a court that drew poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarafa">Tarafa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nabigha-al-dhubyani">al-Nabigha</a>, helping shape classical Arabic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 602 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> deposed and executed al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-numan-iii-ibn-al-mundhir">Nu'man III</a>, the last Lakhmid king, and replaced the dynasty with Persian governors. The loss of this buffer contributed to the Arab victory over Persian forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dhi-qar">Dhi Qar</a> and left the frontier open to the armies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hira">al-Hira</a> in 633.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lakhmid-kingdom.jpg" alt="Lakhmid Kingdom" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: MoeyElan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lakhmid_Kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 CE – 602 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Hira</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Imru al-Qays ibn Amr, al-Mundhir III, al-Nu'man III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arabian polytheism, later Church of the East</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/kuwait">Kuwait</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lakhmids, Kingdom of al-Hira, Banu Lakhm, Nasrids of al-Hira</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=451&amp;focus=lakhmid-kingdom" aria-label="Lakhmid Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5825 1017 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lakhmid Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6145 1273L6357 1235M6145 1273L6219 1701"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6357 1235h0M6219 1701h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6145 1273h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6145 1273h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=451&amp;focus=lakhmid-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></di